ground_truth:
For PATCH update operations, replace ArmResourcePatchAsync with ArmCustomPatchAsync and explicitly set the patch model to Azure.ResourceManager.Foundations.ResourceUpdateModel<Resource, Properties>. ArmResourcePatchAsync is not supported by the JS emitter, and ArmCustomPatchAsync with an explicit ResourceUpdateModel generates the correct update models for JS SDK code and sample generation.

Example pattern:

interface MaintenanceRequests {
  update is ArmCustomPatchAsync<
    MaintenanceRequest,
    PatchModel = Azure.ResourceManager.Foundations.ResourceUpdateModel<
      MaintenanceRequest,
      MaintenanceRequestProperties
    >
  >;
}

interface Supercomputers {
  update is ArmCustomPatchAsync<
    Supercomputer,
    PatchModel = Azure.ResourceManager.Foundations.ResourceUpdateModel<
      Supercomputer,
      SupercomputerProperties
    >
  >;
}

To model a dictionary whose keys are arbitrary strings and whose values may be any JSON value, define the property as an object with unconstrained additional properties:

```json
"extendedProperties": {
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": {}
}
```

Using `additionalProperties: { "type": "object" }` is too restrictive here because it requires each dictionary value to be a JSON object, so values such as strings or string arrays do not match the schema. In practice, AutoRest interprets `{}` as allowing any JSON value. In TypeSpec, the equivalent is `Record<unknown>`.
